      "text": "The claim that the \"Pradhan Mantri Free Laptop Yojana 2026\" is providing free laptops to all students from the government is false. [1] [2] [3] [4] [5] [6] [7] [8] [9] The Press Information Bureau (PIB) Fact Check has repeatedly debunked such claims, clarifying that the Indian government is not running any such scheme. [1] [4] [5] [6] [7] [8] [9] These messages are often scams designed to collect personal information. [4] [10]\n\nWhile there is no central government scheme providing free laptops to all students, some state governments and other organizations do offer laptop schemes or assistance. For example:\n*   The Delhi government provides free laptops to 1200 meritorious students who passed the 10th standard under the 'Mukhyamantri Digital Shiksha Yojana'. [11] [12]\n*   The Tamil Nadu government has a \"Ulagam Ungal Kaiyil\" scheme with a budget of ₹2,000 crore for 2026-27, aiming to cover 10 lakh students. [4]\n*   Uttar Pradesh has the \"Swami Vivekananda Yuva Sashaktikaran Yojana (Mission DigiShakti)\". [4]\n*   Madhya Pradesh offers ₹25,000 for purchasing a laptop to students who score 80% or more in the 12th standard from government or MP Board affiliated schools. [13]\n*   Some non-profit organizations and educational institutions also provide free or discounted laptops to eligible students, often based on financial need or academic performance. [14] [15] [16] [17] [18]\n\nIt is important to verify information about such schemes from official government sources to avoid falling victim to scams. [1] [5] [6] [7] [8] [9]",
